# Flat mode keeps the desktop mouse (#338: only a VR session drops it), and a key during the publisher logos - the
# flat player's way past them - reaches a main menu whose character creation draws completely (#399: without the
# level 10 intro no camera was ever prepared, and the screen showed only the portrait on black). A player's launch
# without VR; New game, two attribute clicks and the camera turn are all done with the desktop mouse, through SDL's
# event queue like a real mouse's (the channel's desktop-mouse); the keys go the same way (desktop-key: @key needs a
# game window, which the CI runner's session does not have). The VR counterpart: desktop-mouse.xrs.
#   .\scripts\dev\xrs-run.ps1 desktop-mouse-flat
@player
@save mvp-new-game
@args --no-vr

# A key during the first logo: straight to the main menu, no level 10 intro
@until state eq LOGO 60s
desktop-key Space down
@frames 5
desktop-key Space up
@log GameEvent:.*startup\.logo name=\w+ state=end how=key 10s
@until state eq MENU 30s
@assert menuMode eq mainmenu
@frames 60
@assert desktopMouse.ignored eq false
@nolog startup\.intro state=start

# New game with the desktop mouse
desktop-mouse at menu newquest
@frames 5
@assert ui.menuHover eq newquest
desktop-mouse button 1 down
@frames 5
desktop-mouse button 1 up
@until menuMode eq charactercreation 15s
@frames 120                                  # the screen fades in
@assert hero.strength eq 6
@assert hero.attributePoints eq 16

# The screen fills the window: the background and the widgets in every quarter, not the portrait on black (#399)
shot window-chargen
@until capture.name eq window-chargen 5s
@assert capture.topLeft.verdict eq world
@assert capture.topRight.verdict eq world
@assert capture.bottomLeft.verdict eq world
@assert capture.bottomRight.verdict eq world

# The left button spends a point on strength, the right one takes it back
desktop-mouse at menu strength
@frames 5
@assert ui.menuHover eq strength
desktop-mouse button 1 down
@frames 5
desktop-mouse button 1 up
@frames 5
@assert hero.strength eq 7
@assert hero.attributePoints eq 15
desktop-mouse button 2 down
@frames 5
desktop-mouse button 2 up
@frames 5
@assert hero.strength eq 6
@assert hero.attributePoints eq 16
@assert desktopMouse.droppedButtons eq 0

# Back to the main menu, and into the game: the mouse turns the camera
desktop-key Escape down
@frames 5
desktop-key Escape up
@until menuMode eq mainmenu 10s
loadslot 0
@until state eq GAMEPLAY 60s
@wait 3000                                   # past the level's fade-in
@assert player.controlsBlocked eq false
@assert player.freelook eq true
@remember yaw player.yaw
desktop-mouse move 300 0
@frames 5
desktop-mouse move 300 0
@frames 10
@assert player.yaw ne {yaw}
@assert desktopMouse.droppedMoves eq 0

# The desktop window's focus keeps the engine's flat behaviour (#406; only VR ignores it, window-focus.xrs): a focus
# loss puts the weapon away and leaves free look (the cursor mode), and the focus coming back does not restore free
# look - the flat player clicks. The focus changes go through SDL's event queue (the channel's window focus
# lost|gained, window-focus.xrs's header); the test run's window starts without the focus (#487), so it is given first
window focus gained
@until window.focused eq true 5s
combat on
@until vr.weapon.combatMode eq true 5s
@assert player.freelook eq true
window focus lost
@until window.focused eq false 5s
@frames 10
@assert player.freelook eq false
@assert vr.weapon.combatMode eq false
window focus gained
@until window.focused eq true 5s
@frames 30
@assert player.freelook eq false
@assert vr.weapon.combatMode eq false
@assert errors eq 0
